  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how colorectal precursor lesions can arise through a feedback loop between epigenetic disruption, mutation, and altered cell behavior. At the center is the epigenome, shown as chromatin with multiple regulatory marks, which is influenced by ageing, stochastic ageing, and environmental factors or carcinogens. These inputs appear to disturb epigenetic control and interact with specific regulators and transcriptional programs, while also promoting mutation and genome instability.\n\nThe diagram emphasizes that epigenetic changes do not act alone: they feed into genome instability and mutations, and these in turn reinforce epigenomic disruption in a positive-feedback cycle. Canonical cancer genes such as APC and p53, along with loss of imprinting and related changes, link this molecular disruption to cellular outcomes. The main biological consequences highlighted are increased proliferation and reduced apoptosis, which together drive expansion of abnormal cells.\n\nUpward arrows indicate broader cancer-associated traits emerging from this altered state, including inflammation, invasion, and metabolic changes. On the right, these combined effects are represented in an intestinal crypt, where a cluster of abnormal cells expands into a visible precursor lesion. Overall, the image presents tumor initiation as a dynamic interaction between ageing, environment, epigenetic dysregulation, and genetic damage.",
